Input: {"question": "When was the Small Astronomy Satellite launched?", "original_source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d00", "start": 0, "end": 146, "text": "Small Astronomy Satellite 3 The Small Astronomy Satellite 3 (SAS 3, also known as SAS-C before launch) was a NASA X-ray astronomy space telescope."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d00", "start": 147, "end": 192, "text": "It functioned from May 7, 1975 to April 1979."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d00", "start": 193, "end": 251, "text": "It covered the X-ray range with four experiments on board."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d00", "start": 252, "end": 406, "text": "The satellite, built by the Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory (APL), was proposed and operated by MIT's Center for Space Research (CSR)."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d00", "start": 407, "end": 548, "text": "It was launched on a Scout vehicle from the Italian San Marco launch platform near Mombasa, Kenya, into a low-Earth, nearly equatorial orbit."}, "D1S6": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d00", "start": 549, "end": 600, "text": "It was also known as Explorer 53, as part of NASA's"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d01", "start": 0, "end": 146, "text": "Small Astronomy Satellite 3 The Small Astronomy Satellite 3 (SAS 3, also known as SAS-C before launch) was a NASA X-ray astronomy space telescope."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d01", "start": 147, "end": 196, "text": "It functioned from January 1, 1970 to April 1979."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d01", "start": 197, "end": 255, "text": "It covered the X-ray range with four experiments on board."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d01", "start": 256, "end": 410, "text": "The satellite, built by the Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory (APL), was proposed and operated by MIT's Center for Space Research (CSR)."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d01", "start": 411, "end": 552, "text": "It was launched on a Scout vehicle from the Italian San Marco launch platform near Mombasa, Kenya, into a low-Earth, nearly equatorial orbit."}, "D2S6": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d01", "start": 553, "end": 613, "text": "It was also known as Explorer 53, as part of NASA's program."}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d02", "start": 0, "end": 105, "text": "Uhuru (satellite) Uhuru was the first satellite launched specifically for the purpose of X-ray astronomy."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d02", "start": 106, "end": 270, "text": "It was also known as the X-ray Explorer Satellite, SAS-A (for \"Small Astronomy Satellite\" A, being first of the three-spacecraft SAS series), SAS 1, or Explorer 42."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d02", "start": 271, "end": 434, "text": "The observatory was launched on December 12, 1970 into an initial orbit of about 560 km apogee, 520 km perigee, 3 degrees inclination, with a period of 96 minutes."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d02", "start": 435, "end": 467, "text": "The mission ended in March 1973."}, "D3S5": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d02", "start": 468, "end": 532, "text": "Uhuru was a scanning mission, with a spin period of ~12 minutes."}, "D3S6": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d02", "start": 533, "end": 598, "text": "It performed the first comprehensive survey of the entire sky for"}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d03", "start": 0, "end": 140, "text": "Small Astronomy Satellite 2 The Small Astronomy Satellite 2, also known also as SAS-2, SAS B or Explorer 48, was a NASA gamma ray telescope."}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d03", "start": 141, "end": 255, "text": "It was launched on 15 November 1972 into the low Earth orbit with a periapsis of 443 km and an apoapsis of 632 km."}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d03", "start": 256, "end": 301, "text": "It completed its observations on 8 June 1973."}, "D4S4": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d03", "start": 302, "end": 469, "text": "SAS 2 was the second in the series of small spacecraft designed to extend the astronomical studies in the X-ray, gamma-ray, ultraviolet, visible, and infrared regions."}, "D4S5": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d03", "start": 470, "end": 575, "text": "The primary objective of the SAS-B was to measure the spatial and energy distribution of primary galactic"}, "D5S1": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d04", "start": 0, "end": 357, "text": "G���\u0010 > �� * �\u0000�� ' & endstream endobj 23354 0 obj < < /Filter/FlateDecode/First 57/Length 419/N 7/Type/ObjStm > > stream h޼��j� @ \u0010�_e�����K @ �� '' xQ * �EA��v�\u00164�D�o�I�\u001a�\u0018Sċ�\u0019fwvϷgXk\u0018\u0010�e ` '�3 ` �d��G �� @ ( G H���\u0018�F\bݮ\u001a�� '' l� X����D �t�O�s '' �AЮS��\b < Q\u0007\u0017�9|| & � �T�y��4c� '�T\u0004��z����l\u0019r�n ( ��0�7\u0004c < W\u0013 $ �2�e��q�.������P�¾P�u� ��z } y� ˲�h-�2�Z ?"}, "D5S2": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d04", "start": 358, "end": 446, "text": "Z�Z�\u000e�e� $ -� '' \u0000�i���� < 98Kj���j�f�� �\\�^0\u0003��\u001a\u0003Gm\u0019�\u001a\u0003 { bp ) ] � � > J�\u0000+醎ҫ�� F # ."}, "D5S3": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0465-417ffc5a3c3d:d04", "start": 447, "end": 576, "text": "# �\u0016���v�701gL�\u0016�\